<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Horn and East Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Ennarea</h1><p class="st-meta">1300 CE – 1710 CE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A gold-rich kingdom of south-western Ethiopia beyond the Gibe river that paid tribute to the emperors and briefly adopted Christianity.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Ennarea emerged by the 14th century among the Gonga-speaking peoples of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gibe-river">Gibe</a> region, related to the people of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-kaffa">Kingdom of Kaffa</a>. Ethiopian chronicles record it as a tributary land from the 15th century, valued as the main source of the gold and civet that reached the highlands and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-sea">Red Sea</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Ennarea was ruled by a sacred king who traded gold, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a>, slaves and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coffee-economy">coffee</a> northward. Its rulers paid tribute in gold to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/ethiopian-empire">Ethiopian Empire</a>, and in the late 16th century, under Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sarsa-dengel">Sarsa Dengel</a>, its king and court were baptised. Portuguese <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuits</a> passed through in the early 17th century, describing it as a major market.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Cut off from the highlands by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oromo-migrations">Oromo migrations</a>, Ennarea was gradually overrun, and in the early 18th century its old dynasty fled south to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-kaffa">Kaffa</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oromo">Oromo</a> rulers later founded <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/limmu-ennarea">Limmu-Ennarea</a> on its former territory.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="kingdom, 1300 CE – 1710 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 72 V40 L36 54 L50 28 L64 54 L78 40 V72 Z"/><path d="M22 80 H78"/><circle cx="50" cy="24" r="3"/><circle cx="22" cy="36" r="2.5"/><circle cx="78" cy="36" r="2.5"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">kingdom</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Horn and East Africa</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:85.57%;width:8.16%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1300 CE – 1710 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Traditional religion, Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Gonga (Bosha)</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Ennarea, Enarya, Inarya</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1505&amp;focus=kingdom-of-ennarea" aria-label="Kingdom of Ennarea on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5771 1871 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kingdom of Ennarea lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6029 1964h0M6021 2127h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6091 2127h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M6091 2127h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Ethiopia. <a href="/atlas?year=1505&amp;focus=kingdom-of-ennarea">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
